  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Snow showers end today, with colder and dryer\nconditions forecast heading into the weekend, then gradual \nwarming through the work week ahead. \n\nAnother embedded shortwave rounding the base of the quasi-\nstationary trough currently over the eastern Conus will continue\nto provide some additional support for upslope snow showers\nacross portions of SE West Virginia, NW North Carolina, and SW\nVirginia. These snow showers look to end by the early to mid\nmorning hours today. As this embedded shortwave makes its way\nover the region, a slight increase in northwesterly 850mb winds\nwill lead to afternoon winds picking up across the region once\nmore, with gusts in the 20-25 mph range expected across mountain\nlocations, and 15-20 mph range expected for Piedmont locations. \n\nTemperatures across the area will be much cooler compared to\nearlier in the month, as the upper level trough remains over\nthe region through Sunday. Lower 500mb heights combined with \nsurface high pressure settling directly over the region by \nMonday night will lead to several nights of ideal radiational \ncooling with clear skies and calm winds. These 500mb heights \ncombined with 850mb temperatures in the -5 to -10 degree Celsius\nrange will keep high temperatures 5-10 degrees below normal for\nthe Piedmont, and 15-20 degrees below normal for locations west\nof the Blue Ridge. Highs each day through the weekend will \nremain in the upper 40s to low 50s in the Piedmont, with 30s and\n40s for mountain locations. Overnight lows look to fall into \nthe 20s and 30s areawide both Saturday and Sunday nights. \n\nBy early next week, a gradual warming trend occurs, with highs \nin the 50s and 60s by Tuesday and into the upper 60s to near 70 \nby Thursday with no rain expected all week.",
